The Bodhisattva <note>original: "Plutishca"</note> who practiced the ten virtues;
50. He who, in the Legend of Rukmavati <note>original: "Rukmavatya-avadana"</note>, gave his own body to the tigress;
51. The King Adinapunya <gloss>Unyielding Merit</gloss> who became the kinsman of all supplicants;
52. The King who, in his quest for eloquent sayings, purchased a single verse with his own life;
53. He who took away the suffering of all who were tormented by disease, and was thus called the Medicine of Sentient Beings <note>original: "Sattvau-shadhi"</note>;
54. He who gave his own body to protect a bird <note>referring to King Shibi</note>, and became the giver of all;
55. He who pacified the naga <gloss>serpent deity</gloss> Gopala merely by his gaze;
56. Through whose power the earth was marked with jeweled <term>chaityas</term> <gloss>stupas or reliquaries</gloss>;
57. Punyabala <gloss>Strength of Merit</gloss>, who was the kinsman protecting those afflicted by illness;
58. He who was the ethical Kunala;
59. He who pacified the nagas;
60. The prosperous farmer;
61. He who was peaceful and known as Yashoda <gloss>Giver of Fame</gloss>;
62. He who performed the act of favor for Kashyapa;
